{-
   An overload set that is a *set* - `doc/spec/overloading.md`'s R1 through R5.

   R1 keys the set by `(name, arity)` and says what is in it: every class function of that name and
   arity, plus **at most one** plain function. So a name is not one declaration, and every rule below
   is about which member of the set a call reaches.

   What makes this a fixture rather than a note is that the answer is a *number*: each call below
   reaches one of two bodies that return different values, so a selection rule that broke would
   change what the program computes rather than whether it compiles. Both resolvers run it and both
   are held to the same total.

   The live case in the library is `min` and `max`: `Core.yana` declares them as members of `Ord`
   over two `a`s, and `Core/Vector.yana` declares plain ones over `Vec(a, n)` beside them, because a
   comparison of two vectors answers a *mask* and `Ord` is therefore not what describes ordering
   them. This is that shape written small enough to run on a target with no vector opcodes.
-}

class Wide(a):
  fn span(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> Int

data Box {n: Int}
data Tag {n: Int}

instance Wide(Box): fn span(lhs: Box, rhs: Box) -> Int = lhs.n + rhs.n
instance Wide(Tag): fn span(lhs: Tag, rhs: Tag) -> Int = lhs.n * rhs.n

{-
   R5 - a plain function joins the set rather than shadowing it.

   "If a plain function matches, it wins over any class candidate... If it does not match,
   resolution continues into the class candidates." Both halves are asserted below: `span(1, 2)`
   reaches this, and `span` of two boxes reaches `Wide(Box)` although this declaration exists and
   has the same name and arity.

   The alternative reading - a plain function *shadowing* the name - is what the rule exists to
   refuse: it would silently disable `Wide`'s member for every type in the module, reported as an
   argument-type error at a call the author never touched.
-}
fn span(lhs: Int, rhs: Int) -> Int = lhs - rhs

{-
   R3 - in generic code the constraints in scope decide.

   Two classes declare `weigh` at one arity, so neither call below can be decided by the argument:
   `a` is a type variable and no instance exists to break the tie. What decides it is the
   requirement the *enclosing signature* declared, which is Haskell's rule without defaulting and
   has the property the spec names - adding a class to a signature can fix an ambiguity rather than
   only ever adding an obligation.

   `Reject.Overload.yana` beside this is the other half: the same body with neither class required.
-}
class Light(a): fn weigh(value: a) -> Int
class Heavy(a): fn weigh(value: a) -> Int

instance Light(Box): fn weigh(value: Box) -> Int = value.n
instance Heavy(Box): fn weigh(value: Box) -> Int = value.n * 100

fn (Light(a)) lightly(value: a) -> Int = weigh(value)
fn (Heavy(a)) heavily(value: a) -> Int = weigh(value)

{-
   R4 - ambiguity is resolved by qualification, never by a tiebreak.

   `Light.weigh(b)` names the class in the same form a namespaced type method uses, and it is what
   the ambiguity diagnostic tells an author to write - so it has to work, and this is where that is
   asserted. Without it an overloaded name would be a dead end rather than a set.
-}
fn qualified(value: Box) -> Int = Heavy.weigh(value) + Light.weigh(value)

{-
   R1's other half, which the old table also could not express: two *classes* declaring one member
   name and arity, with no plain function anywhere near it. Nothing in `lib/` writes this, and the
   standard library declining to is a policy rather than a language rule - so the mechanism is
   asserted here.
-}
fn main() -> Int:
    -- R5, both directions. 1 - 2 is -1; the boxes reach `Wide(Box)`, which adds.
    let plain = span(1, 2)
    let classed = span(Box {n: 4}, Box {n: 5})
    let other = span(Tag {n: 3}, Tag {n: 6})

    -- R3, at a type both classes have an instance for, so only the requirement decides.
    let light = lightly(Box {n: 7})
    let heavy = heavily(Box {n: 7})

    -- R4.
    let named = qualified(Box {n: 2})

    return plain + classed + other + light + heavy + named
